ited the injured together at two hospitals the following day — a carefully managed unity display. Among those who helped subdue Mr El Koudri were Egyptian nationals; Mr Tajani proposed a civilian bravery medal for all of them.\n\nThis is now the third documented instance in the current period where the two deputy prime ministers have publicly diverged on migration and security in terms visible to international audiences. The pattern is consistent enough to read as a governing mechanism: Salvini escalates, Mr Tajani corrects, Meloni holds studied ambiguity. Meloni cancelled a scheduled bilateral with Cyprus to attend the Modena hospitals — a defensible call given the severity of the attack, but one that illustrates the cost of governing at this level of compound pressure: diplomatic commitments are now the first thing cut when domestic management demands attention on short notice.",
